1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,180
You'll see headlines
saying surgical robots

2
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:04,480
are now 80-something percent autonomous.

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:05,840
Good, that number is real.

4
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:08,200
But it's also misleading. Let me explain.

5
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:13,400
In experiments, robots have completed most
steps of certain procedures on their own.

6
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:15,320
Impressive, real, worth celebrating.

7
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:17,800
But here's what that percentage hides.

8
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:21,040
The steps a robot can
automate are predictable ones.

9
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:23,360
The remaining part,
the unexpected bleeding,

10
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,820
the anatomy that doesn't
match the textbook,

11
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:28,760
the split-second judgment call,
that's the hard part.

12
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:33,020
And it's exactly the part that decides
whether a patient lives or dies.

13
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:38,080
Automating the easy 90% doesn't
get you 90% of the way there.

14
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,000
The last 10% is the whole game.

15
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:42,360
So, when you hear mostly autonomous,

16
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:45,700
translate it as the machine
can handle the routine

17
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:49,380
and the surgeon is still essential
for everything that actually matters.

18
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:50,960
That's not a limitation to hide.

19
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,700
It's the reason surgery is safe.

20
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,580
The hardest part
of medicine is the human part.

21
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:57,720
Medicine? Ask Cratheon.
